413→                           The Fibonacci sequence $1,1,2,3,5,\ldots$ shows that $m=1$ and $n=2$ is possible. The sequence of powers of $2$ shows that $m=0$ and $n=1$ is possible. The case $m=2$ and $n=3$ is not known.<br><br>van Doorn <a href="https://github.com/Woett/Mathematical-shorts/blob/main/Any%20multiset%20which%20is%20complete%20after%20removing%20any%20two%20elements%20remains%20complete%20after%20removing%20a%20specific%20infinite%20set.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">has shown</a> that no such sequence exists for $2\leq m&lt;n$ if we interpret complete in the strong sense that\[\left\{ \sum_{n\in B}n : \textrm{ for all finite }B\subset A\right\}=\mathbb{N}.\]Erdős and Graham most likely meant the weaker notion of completeness which allows finitely many exceptions, however.
